<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/intermediate/senses-photons-to-perception">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You now hold the full cellular toolkit: neurons, synapses, spikes. This lesson runs it through
    the sensory systems — the machinery that turns physics into experience. The deep point is that
    the brain never touches the world directly: every sense converts a physical quantity — photons,
    pressure waves, chemical concentrations, tissue strain — into the one currency it can read,
    electrical spikes. Different sensors, identical output language. And because perception is a
    construction from those sparse, noisy spike trains, what you experience is a model rather than
    a recording — Anil Seth calls it "controlled hallucination," and the experiments that prove it
    (blind spots, illusions, change blindness) are the seams of the model showing. We will tour the
    senses in order of cortical real estate: vision first, then hearing, the chemical senses, and
    the body senses that hold the whole model together.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the senses, from physical signal to constructed experience" widget="equationStep" config={sensesCfg} />

  <h2>Transduction: the universal trick</h2>
  <p>
    Every sensory system begins with a <strong>receptor</strong> — a cell specialised to convert its
    physical quantity into a change of membrane voltage. The mechanisms are as varied as the
    physics: stretch-sensitive ion channels that open when the membrane is deformed (touch),
    ligand-gated receptors that bind odorant molecules (smell), photon-activated protein cascades
    (vision), cilia bent by fluid motion (hearing, balance). But the output is always the same
    shape: a graded <strong>receptor potential</strong> whose size tracks stimulus intensity, which
    — past threshold — becomes spike frequency in the afferent nerve. The nervous system also
    wires each receptor to a fixed address: activity in a given fibre is always interpreted as
    <em>that fibre's</em> modality and location, no matter what triggered it — the <strong>labelled
    line</strong> principle (press on your closed eye and the "light" you see is your visual system
    dutifully reporting activity arriving on optic-nerve lines). One more universal property:
    <strong>adaptation</strong>. Receptors reduce their firing under sustained stimulation — which
    is why you stop feeling your watch, why a smell fades in minutes, and why the nervous system
    prioritises <em>change</em> over steady state: evolution budgets attention for news, not
    wallpaper.
  </p>

  <h2>Vision I: the eye and the retina</h2>
  <p>
    Vision begins as optics: the cornea and lens focus light onto the <strong>retina</strong>, a
    sheet of neural tissue — developmentally a bit of brain, sitting in the eye — holding about
    120 million <strong>rods</strong> and 6 million <strong>cones</strong>. Rods are exquisitely
    sensitive single-photon detectors for dim light, concentrated in the periphery (why faint
    stars vanish when you look straight at them); cones come in three spectral types (short,
    medium, long wavelength) packed into the fovea and are what daylight vision and colour run on.
    The transduction is one of biology's great amplifiers: a single photon isomerises one molecule
    of the pigment retinal inside one rhodopsin protein, which — through a G-protein cascade —
    changes the fate of hundreds of downstream molecules, each triggering further steps, until the
    receptor potential represents the catch with a gain of millions. Colour, note carefully, does
    not exist in the light: wavelengths do. Colour is the brain's model built from <em>comparing</em>
    the three cone responses — a three-number summary of the light's spectrum, which is why two
    physically different spectra can look identical (metamerism, the principle every screen
    exploits with just three sub-pixel colours). Before anything reaches the brain, the retina
    computes: its interneurons pool photoreceptor outputs into <strong>centre-surround
    antagonism</strong> — ganglion cells that fire to a bright centre with a dark surround — which
    emphasises edges and contrast over raw illumination (the opposite that makes shadows look
    darker than physics says, the Hermann grid's grey dots, and the optimised compression of
    images into ~1.5 million ganglion-cell axons, a hundredfold reduction from receptor count).
    And the optics have a flaw worth knowing: where the optic nerve exits, there are no receptors —
    the <strong>blind spot</strong>. You never see it because the brain interpolates across the
    hole: perception is construction, demonstrated on your own retina.
  </p>

  <h2>Vision II: cortex, maps and streams</h2>
  <p>
    Ganglion-cell axons run the optic nerve, cross partially at the optic chiasm (so each
    hemisphere sees the opposite visual field), and land in the thalamus, which relays to
    <strong>primary visual cortex (V1)</strong> at the occipital pole. V1 is a map — cortical
    position tracks retinal position (retinotopy) — but its cells answer more specific questions:
    Hubel and Wiesel (Nobel 1981) found neurons that respond only to edges of particular
    <em>orientation</em> in particular places, and built the hierarchy that follows: from edge
    detectors in V1 to cells in later areas that respond to motion direction, to faces, to
    whole scenes. From V1, traffic splits into two streams: the <strong>ventral</strong> ("what")
    stream, running down toward the temporal lobe, identifying objects, faces and text; and the
    <strong>dorsal</strong> ("where/how") stream, up toward the parietal lobe, computing location,
    motion and the coordinates that guide reaching. The split is clinically visible: damage to the
    ventral stream can leave a patient who cannot recognise a face yet navigates the room
    perfectly, and vice versa. Scale the whole enterprise and you see why vision dominates: an
    estimated third to half of primate cortex is vision-related — sight is not a channel among
    several but the brain's primary input pipe.
  </p>

  <h2>Hearing: a physical frequency analyser</h2>
  <p>
    Sound is pressure oscillation; the ear's job is to turn a range of roughly 20 Hz to 20 kHz
    into spike patterns. The outer ear funnels; the eardrum vibrates; three tiny ossicle bones
    leverage the vibration ~20-fold and deliver it to the fluid-filled <strong>cochlea</strong>,
    where the magic happens mechanically: the basilar membrane runs the length of the coil,
    <em>stiff and narrow at the base, loose and wide at the apex</em> — so each frequency maximally
    vibrates one place along it, high frequencies at the base, low at the apex. Sitting on that
    membrane are <strong>hair cells</strong> (~3,500 inner ones doing the reporting, ~12,000 outer
    ones doing something extraordinary): bending their stereocilia opens ion channels, converting
    vibration to receptor potential, and the auditory nerve reads the whole map — every frequency
    has its own fibre, the <strong>tonotopic</strong> principle, preserved all the way to cortex
    (the auditory cortex is literally a map of pitch). The outer hair cells are the surprise:
    they are motor cells that physically amplify the membrane's motion a hundredfold, an active
    process that makes the ear the most sensitive force detector in the body (thresholds near the
    vibration of atoms) and produces the faint otoacoustic emissions that newborn hearing screens
    listen for. The system's one great weakness: mammalian hair cells do not regenerate. Loud
    noise, ototoxic drugs and ageing kill them permanently — the reason hearing loss is one-way
    and why noise protection is actual neuroprotection.
  </p>

  <h2>The chemical senses: taste and smell</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Taste</strong> is the gatekeeper sense — deciding accept or reject before swallowing —
    and it is coarse by design: five receptor families (sweet, salty, sour, bitter, umami)
    distributed across tongue and throat, each a rough proxy for a biological question (energy,
    electrolytes, danger of spoilage, protein). Richness of "flavour" is mostly smell operating
    retronasally — which is why food is cardboard when a cold blocks the nose.
    <strong>Olfaction</strong> is the opposite: the most ancient, most direct sensory highway.
    Humans carry about 400 types of olfactory receptor; each neuron expresses one type; and the
    system's trick is <strong>combinatorial coding</strong> — a single odorant molecule activates
    a characteristic subset of the 400, so the space of possible subset-activations is
    astronomically larger than 400. Estimates of discriminable odours from this logic have run to
    a trillion or more (the famous 2014 figure, since debated in the literature), but the
    principle is settled: hundreds of receptors, combinatorial binding, effectively unlimited
    vocabulary. Olfaction is also anatomically unique — its nerve projects almost directly into
    cortex and limbic structures (amygdala, hippocampus) without passing through the thalamus —
    the circuit-level reason smells trigger memory and emotion more forcefully than sights.
    Adaptation is dramatic here (any odorant fades within minutes) and the system remains the
    senses' most underrated: humans test reliably better than dogs at some follow-a-trail tasks
    and can detect some compounds at parts per trillion.
  </p>

  <h2>The body senses: touch, pain, balance</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Somatosensation</strong> is a family of channels, not one sense: distinct
    mechanoreceptors in skin report light touch, vibration (Pacinian corpuscles), stretch and
    indentation, each with its own precision — two-point discrimination on the fingertip resolves
    about 2–3 mm, on the back about 40 mm, a 15-fold map distortion mirrored exactly in the
    somatosensory homunculus. <strong>Proprioception</strong> — muscle spindles and joint
    receptors reporting limb position — runs constantly below awareness; lose it (as the
    neurologist Jonathan Cole's patient I.W. did) and the body must be relearned by sight, every
    movement consciously supervised. <strong>Pain</strong> is not an over-flying touch sense but
    a parallel system: dedicated nociceptors for mechanical, thermal and chemical insult, running
    slow fibres, gated in the spinal cord (Melzack and Wall's 1965 gate-control theory — competing
    touch signals can close the gate, hence rubbing a knock) and heavily modulated top-down
    (endorphins, placebo analgesia, the analgesia soldiers report in combat). Chronic pain is the
    system's failure mode: the alarm itself becomes the disease. And <strong>vestibular</strong>
    sense — semicircular canals sensing angular acceleration through inertia of their fluid, and
    otolith organs sensing linear acceleration and head tilt — anchors balance, stabilises gaze,
    and contributes the motion-sickness conflict signal when vision and vestibulum disagree.
    Multimodal fusion is the rule everywhere: the McGurk effect (seeing "ga" over hearing "ba"
    yields the perception "da") shows the senses fuse <em>before</em> awareness, not after.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The expert layer begins with <strong>prediction</strong>. The modern framework — predictive
    processing — holds that the cortex continuously generates a top-down model of incoming
    sensory causes and only the <em>prediction errors</em> climb the hierarchy; perception is the
    model, corrected by error signals, which explains why context so easily overrides stimulus
    (the McGurk fusion, the hollow-mask illusion, pain's sensitivity to expectation) and why the
    brain fills the blind spot without being asked. Then, <strong>what illusions prove</strong>:
    every illusion is a natural experiment on the construction — change blindness and
    inattentional blindness (Simons and Chabris's invisible gorilla, 1999) show how little of the
    scene is actually encoded (attention is the bottleneck, not the eye); colour constancy shows
    the brain discounting the illuminant to report object properties; the rubber-hand illusion
    shows the body model itself is a construction updated by correlated evidence. Next,
    <strong>sensory substitution</strong>: feed camera data to the tongue as electrical patterns,
    or teach blind users human echolocation, and the cortex — particularly visual areas — learns
    to read it, evidence that cortex is a general-purpose statistical machine whose "visual" and
    "auditory" labels are about wiring, not essence. And finally the honest census: the canonical
    "five senses" is a children's list. Proprioception, vestibular sense, temperature
    (thermoreception), pain (nociception) and interoception (the body's internal state — hunger,
    visceral stretch, even the sense of heartbeat now measurable as cortical events) are distinct
    modalities with distinct receptors, and interoception's recent promotion to a first-class
    research programme is quietly reshaping theories of emotion itself. Perception, in the end,
    is the whole organism's running model of the world and of itself — built by transducers, not
    by truth.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Seth, Being You (2021); Gregory, Eye and Brain; Hubel &amp; Wiesel (Nobel 1981); Bushdid et al., Science (2014) and critiques; Simons &amp; Chabris, "Gorillas in Our Midst" (1999).</p>
</TopicPage>
